# PATCH: go-mitmproxy v1.9.2 origin-transport hook

This directory is a verbatim copy of `github.com/lqqyt2423/go-mitmproxy` at tag
**v1.9.2** (module hash `h1:+QTbDyUUaVRmEavPq0/eWzJ2K+K4JdL4ff91s/adnxk=`, recorded
in `m1nervac0nsole/go.sum`) with the hunks below applied. `LICENSE` is the
upstream MIT license, unchanged. `m1nervac0nsole/go.mod` replaces the module
with this directory; the module cache is never edited.

Why a source copy: v1.9.2 exposes no seam for the origin dial, the origin TLS
handshake or the origin round trip, and an addon cannot refuse a CONNECT before
the dial or take over a WebSocket upgrade. See
`m1nervac0nsole/docs/design/network-boundary-implementation-plan.md`, "Resolved
decisions" item 1.

Behaviour with `Options.OriginTransport == nil` is upstream v1.9.2 except where a
hunk says otherwise: Hunk 8 is an independent defect fix, and Hunks 9-10 change
WebSocket addon delivery for every configuration (an addon that does not implement
the optional interface sees exactly the hooks it saw before).

## Tests

The module's own tests are not a gate: no CI job runs them, and two of them fail
under Go 1.26 for upstream reasons that have nothing to do with the hunks below —
`cert.TestNewCA` compares a generated CA PEM against a golden, and
`proxy.TestProxy/test_https_server/should_generate_not_trusted_error` asserts the
raw `x509.UnknownAuthorityError` that Go now wraps in a `tls` error. Do not
"fix" them by editing the golden: the hook is covered by
`m1nervac0nsole/traffic/` and by the guest integration tests, which is the
coverage that matters.

## Applied hunks

### Hunk 1 — `proxy/proxy.go`: the hook and its error type

- `Options.OriginTransport OriginTransport` (nil keeps upstream behaviour) and
  `Options.MaxStreamEvents int` (0 keeps every event).
- New `OriginTransport` interface: `Dial`, `Handshake`, `RoundTrip`, each taking
  `*ConnContext` so an implementation can key its own per-connection state.
- New `OriginError{Stage, Status, Reason, Header}` with `Error()`. An
  implementation returns it from `Dial`/`Handshake` so the CONNECT reply carries
  the stage, and `Header` (when set) carries what the client needs to answer the
  refusal, such as a `Proxy-Authenticate` challenge.
- New `DialUpstream(ctx, proxyURL, address, sslInsecure)`, the exported form of
  the existing exit dial, so a hook reuses the maintained http/https/socks5
  logic instead of copying it. `getUpstreamConn` now delegates to it.
- New `(*Proxy).originTransport()`.

### Hunk 2 — `proxy/attacker.go`: dial, handshake, round trip

- `httpsDial`: `ot.Dial(ctx, connCtx, req)` when the hook is set, otherwise
  `getUpstreamConn` unchanged. The rest (ServerConn, `ServerConnected`) is
  untouched.
- `serverTlsHandshake`: `ot.Handshake(ctx, connCtx, serverConn.Conn,
  clientHello.ServerName)` when the hook is set, otherwise the existing
  `tls.Client` block unchanged. `serverConn.tlsConn` is assigned from either
  path, `TlsEstablishedServer` still fires, and the existing
  `serverConn.client` is still built for the nil-hook path only.
- `attack`: the hook's `RoundTrip` is tried first, so both the cross-authority
  client (`useSeparateClient`) and the per-connection client go through one
  policy-checked call site. Everything after it (Responseheaders, SSE
  detection, buffering, `StreamResponseModifier`, reply) is unchanged.

### Hunk 3 — `proxy/entry.go`: dial-first, error attribution, tunnel dial

- `handleConnect`: `ClientConn.UpstreamCert || originTransport() != nil` selects
  `httpsDialFirstAttack`, so a later `NewUpstreamCertAddon(false)` cannot
  reintroduce the lazy path's "200 before any dial" regression when a hook is
  active.
- `httpsDialFirstAttack`: a failed dial is reported by `writeOriginError`, which
  writes `OriginError.Status` (default 502), `OriginError.Header` and
  `X-M1nervac0nsole-Gateway: <stage>`; an unclassified error still yields 502.
  Without the header copy a refusal could not carry a `Proxy-Authenticate`
  challenge, and a browser would never send its proxy credential.
- `directTransfer`: dials through `dialOrigin`, which uses the hook when set, so
  a non-intercepted tunnel cannot skip policy.
- New `dialOrigin` and `writeOriginError`; `errors` imported. New
  `proxy/origin_error_test.go` covers the reply this function writes.

### Hunk 4 — `proxy/connection.go`: `tlsConn` type

- `ServerConn.tlsConn` is `net.Conn` instead of `*tls.Conn`, because a uTLS
  connection is not a `*tls.Conn`. The only reader is `serveConn`'s h2
  `DialTLSContext`, which already accepts `net.Conn`.

### Hunk 5 — `proxy/websocket.go`: WSS reuses the CONNECT connection

- `handleWSS` reuses `connCtx.ServerConn.tlsConn` when the CONNECT already
  dialed and handshook the origin. Before this, the handler dialed a second
  origin connection, overwrote `connCtx.ServerConn` without closing the first,
  and ran a second TLS handshake with `InsecureSkipVerify` from `SslInsecure`.
  When no connection exists it dials through the hook (`Dial` + `Handshake`) or
  `getUpstreamConn`, and the handshake state is reported to addons.
- The gorilla dialer uses `NetDialTLSContext`, which tells gorilla the TLS
  handshake is already done, so it neither re-handshakes nor consults
  `TLSClientConfig`.
- `context` imported.

### Hunk 6 — `proxy/flow.go`, `proxy/sse.go`: bounded stream retention

- `WebSocketData.Dropped` / `SSEData.Dropped` count what `MaxStreamEvents`
  discarded, so a reader can tell a short stream from a truncated one.
- `addMessage` and `addEvent` take the limit and stop appending past it. With
  `MaxStreamEvents == 0` nothing changes.
- The two `addEvent` call sites in `proxy/sse.go` pass
  `proxy.Opts.MaxStreamEvents`.

Without this, a long-lived WebSocket or SSE session grows `f.WebScoket.Messages`
/ `f.SSE.Events` without bound, which the plan's bounded-memory rule forbids.

### Hunk 7 — `proxy/addon.go`: empty-retention guard

- `LogAddon.WebSocketMessage` returns when no message is retained. It indexed
  the last element unconditionally, which panics once Hunk 6 drops a message.

### Hunk 8 — `proxy/websocket.go`: WebSocket subprotocol

Independent of the hook, and present in v1.9.2: `cloneHeaderWithoutWSHandshake`
deletes `Sec-Websocket-Protocol` (the gorilla dialer rejects it as a duplicate)
and `Upgrader.Subprotocols` was never set, so both the origin and the client saw
a successful upgrade with no negotiated subprotocol.

- `handle` and `handleWSS` pass the client's subprotocol list to the dialer and
  echo the origin's choice back through `Upgrader.Subprotocols`.

### Hunk 9 — `proxy/addon.go`: optional WebSocket recorder extension

Additive to the `Addon` interface, which is unchanged, so `BaseAddon`,
`LogAddon`, `web.WebAddon` and every other implementation compile untouched.

- New optional `WebSocketRecorder` interface: `WebSocketFrame(f, m)` and
  `WebSocketClosed(f, fromClient, err)`. `WebSocketMessage(*Flow)` carries no
  message, and the flow's retained list is capped by `MaxStreamEvents` and
  appended from both forwarding goroutines, so an addon can neither identify the
  current message nor rely on it still being retained. The extension hands the
  message itself over, before it is forwarded.
- New `WebSocketCloseError` alias for `websocket.CloseError`, so an addon can
  record a closure's code and reason without importing gorilla itself.

### Hunk 10 — `proxy/flow.go`, `proxy/websocket.go`: ordered delivery

- `WebSocketData.addMessage` returns the message it built, including one dropped
  by `MaxStreamEvents`, so a recorder sees every frame whatever the flow retains.
- `forwardMessages` type-asserts each addon for `WebSocketRecorder` and delivers
  the frame before forwarding it. Each direction reports its close once, through a
  deferred `WebSocketClosed` that runs before the direction signals completion.
- `forwardMessages` drains both directions before the deferred `WebSocketEnd`
  instead of returning on the first one. Previously the second direction could
  still be delivering when `WebSocketEnd` ran, so an addon finalizing there could
  miss frames. The first direction's deferred close already unblocks the second,
  so the drain cannot hang. The first error is still the one returned.
- New `closeErr`, `frame` and `closed` helpers; `closeErr` keeps the previous
  return value (a normal close is not an error).

## Re-applying on an upstream bump

The hunks are small and local. On a new upstream tag: copy the module, then
re-apply in the order above and confirm with

    go build github.com/lqqyt2423/go-mitmproxy/proxy/
    go test github.com/lqqyt2423/go-mitmproxy/proxy/

The dependency's own `TestProxy/test_https_server/should_generate_not_trusted_error`
asserts a Go error string (`certificate is not trusted`) that current Go no
longer produces; it fails identically on the unpatched v1.9.2 tree, so it is not
a regression from this patch.
